 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel relative to the number of primitives, as it relies on fixed-function rasterization hardware [G]. The cost is dominated by the GPU fill rate [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires a framebuffer for the intersection coordinates; no additional acceleration structures are needed for the intersection test itself [G].
-*   **Performance:** Highly efficient on modern GPUs due to parallel rasterization [S1]. It avoids the branching and arithmetic overhead of analytical ray-box intersection tests [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; leverages existing graphics pipeline; fast for simple bounding volumes [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Low precision due to framebuffer resolution and bit depth; only suitable for convex, simple bounding volumes; cannot handle complex internal geometry or multiple intersections without additional passes [G].
-*   **When to use:** When a quick estimate of the entry point for a simple volume is needed, and high precision is not critical [G].
-*   **When not to use:** For complex non-convex volumes, or when high-precision intersection points are required for accurate sampling [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel, dominated by rasterization overhead.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Low; requires only vertex buffers and a framebuffer.
+*   **Performance:** Highly efficient on GPUs due to parallel rasterization.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, leverages existing GPU hardware, works for any projection type.
+*   **Limitations:** Limited by framebuffer precision, requires two passes for entry/exit, not suitable for complex non-convex volumes.
 
 ## Background
-This technique is described in comparative studies of GPU-based volume ray casting implementations, highlighting rasterization as a viable alternative to compute-shader or CUDA-based analytical intersection methods [S1]. It utilizes standard OpenGL rasterization capabilities to offload the intersection calculation to the fixed-function pipeline [S1].
+Originated as a technique to accelerate volume ray casting by replacing explicit ray-box intersection tests with hardware rasterization [S1]. It is commonly used in GPU-based volume rendering implementations [S1].
 
